able  and so the there's been a great  scholarly effort it through the Beauvoir  research series which is published by  the university of illinois press over  the last couple of decades and there's  so but even the second sex which is the  book that she's most famous for was  translated without a very philosophical  approach and published in 1953 and that  translation shaped the reception of  Beauvoir in the english-speaking world  until 2009 when a new translation  appeared the first one actually only  included about 85% of the original  French text it excluded her discussion  of housework it excluded a lot of  discussions of how women in their own  voice so it was it was whatever the  intentions of the translator the message  of the book was quite altered by the  translation that had at first in English  and even in french-speaking countries  with French texts there was presumably  still this presumption that Beauvoir  relied on Sartre oh like that that she  was shaped by her partner in infrasonic  lis yes no I wouldn't deny that she was  shaped by her partner philosophically  but I think the problem is that the that  kind of admission usually only goes one  way and sart's philosophy was very much  shaped by her commitments and her  questions and her challenges to what he  published but the problem is that  because Beauvoir wasn't translated until  much later what came into english was a  lot of sart's philosophy and her  contributions to the french  existentialism were widely overlooked  especially in the popular sphere do you  think time lags like that which have a  big impact on public perception are gone  now in a digital age do you think that  could happen  again well I haven't yet seen a couple  that has a contender for sergeant  Beauvoir and as public intellectuals in  but I think translation is still very  difficult to achieve and it's it seems  to be the case that it's fewer women are  translated than men and so I think you  know the times have changed but I'm not  convinced that they've changed as much  as they need to we're spreading women's  work or intellectual work is  acknowledged as it should be so okay  so why decide to write about Beauvoir's  life rather than about her writing so  the decision to write about her life was  not an easy one to make but one of ours  points in the second sex is that women  are often judged as successes or  failures on the basis of their romantic  life they're often reduced to their  romantic lives and what what I have  found partly through my research on  Sarge because I worked on sari before  Beauvoir was that there were a lot of  French philosophical sources informing  his early philosophy that English  speakers didn't know about and so they  didn't can hear the conversations that  he was having with the French  philosophical tradition in texts like  being nothingness and when I read both  of our student Diaries which cover the  period from 1926 to 1930 which  importantly includes a few years before  sight was even in the picture I found  that she was preoccupied by themes that  would later be called existentialist  before she even met him and that they  were reading many of the same neglected  sources and so the idea that these ideas  were his in the first place was  problematic and once I started to look  at the way both were described their  relationship in the different volumes of  her autobiography which was published  over twelve years between 1958 and 1972  um I saw that she had noticed the way  her successes were attributed to Sarge  so she was called Sark's disciple she  was accused of popularizing his ideas  she didn't live to see the obituary that  called her as imagination list as her  inkwell but she lived to see a lot of  reviews of her books where the content  of her novels or her philosophical works  were attributed to Sartre and she was  just called effectively a good  communicator that's what women are good  at communicating not thinking they just  know how to when someone else has done  the thinking to communicate it well so  once I saw that pattern emerge in the  reception of her works I became  extremely suspicious of a lot of the  existing scholarly literature about  Beauvoir in English and I thought what  happens if we read the rest of her life  on the basis of the philosophy that she  was developing as a young woman and I  found that there's a lot of continuity  there and that um you started has been  given way more credit than he deserves  yeah and so speaking maybe a bit more  about her philosophy what might Beauvoir  say about contemporary discourses  surrounding issues like gender identity  and biological essentialism yes so she  clearly rejects biological essentialism  but that term has taken on many meanings  in the contemporary context which were  not necessarily present in the same  senses in 1949 and one of the one of the  interesting things about the second sex  now is that many people think on the  basis of its most famous sentence where  before says that when is born oh sorry  one is not born but rather becomes a  woman that she's denying the relevance  of Anatomy to what it means to be a  woman but that sentence actually is a  almost verbatim quotation from a French  philosopher who wrote about the freedom  and determinism debate and then the late  1890s called Alfred who a  and he said that one is not born but  rather becomes free and bovar was an  existentialist philosopher who was  committed to the idea that freedom is  something that you constantly have to  strive for in your life and the she  thought that women were not encouraged  to pursue their own values and to pursue  their own freedom in the same ways that  men were as a as a result of childhood  so she says in the second sex what she's  trying to investigate is what humanity  has made of the human female and she  thinks that culture and childhood play a  really important role in the oppression  of women and even the feelings of  division and dissatisfaction that women  have within themselves and how about  then existentialism and feminism are  they compatible yes but what is  existentialism is very much compatible  with her feminism and I think one of the  things that I find fascinating as a  philosopher is how she didn't know she  did not write the second sex as an  activist book so there's a 1975  interview where Beauvoir says she wrote  it as a theorem theoretical work and she  was surprised by the activist role that  went on to play there's a lot of  philosophical work happening in this in  the second sex but but one of the things  that I think is so interesting is that  she's looking at the challenges that  existentialist in general are concerned  with about how you become a self how you  become an ethical self and she's saying  there are challenges confronting women  in becoming ethical cells which are not  identical to the challenges confronting  men and so I think they're compatible  and part of the work of the second sex  is to show how okay cool and I think  it's probably implicit but um how well  'van is she and her philosophy today  so I mean I have been this this my  biography of Babar has been out for a  month tomorrow and I had been astonished  by the the number of people who have  said to me how much it spoke into the  present situation so I think there are  strong progress narratives in feminism  and a tendency to think that women of a  generation before or two generations  before are no longer relevant  but there are some questions in human  life like how to love ethically and what  it means to become yourself given  certain constraints that just don't date  and I think Beauvoir is a philosopher  who spoke to those  you

